<div style="border: 2px solid #1a5276; padding: 15px; text-align: center; background-color: #e0f2f7; margin-bottom: 20px;">
    <h1 style="color: #1a5276; margin: 0;">Lesson Planner – Theme # The Animals in Our Waters – Lesson # 4</h1>
    <p style="color: #1a5276; margin: 5px 0 0 0;">Complete this planner five times per theme, once per lesson (Lesson 1 through Lesson 5).</p>
</div>

<table style="border: 1px dashed #333; border-collapse: collapse; width: 100%; margin: 10px 0;">
    <tr style="border: 1px dashed #333;">
        <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;"><b>Lesson #:</b> 4</td>
        <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;"><b>Skills Focus:</b> Writing</td>
    </tr>
    <tr style="border: 1px dashed #333;">
        <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;"><b>Grade:</b> 9th Grade (CEFR: A2.4)</td>
        <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;"><b>Scenario:</b> Ocean Life and Conservation</td>
        <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;"><b>Theme:</b> The Animals in Our Waters</td>
    </tr>
    <tr style="border: 1px dashed #333;">
        <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;"><b>Date(s):</b> From ___ to ___</td>
        <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;" colspan="2"><b>Learning Sequence Time:</b> 45–50 min</td>
    </tr>
    <tr style="border: 1px dashed #333;">
        <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;" colspan="3">
            <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Specific Objective:</b> Writing: Productive: Write a formal letter to request information. Interactive: Create a collaborative proposal for a class project.</p>
            <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Learning Outcome:</b> Writing: Can compose a formal letter requesting information about local marine conservation initiatives in Panama (e.g., "Request details about volunteering for an ocean cleanup event at Veracruz Beach or participating in a coral reef restoration project at Isla Iguanas.")
            Can contribute to a group project description on protecting marine ecosystems (e.g., "We should mention how protecting marine life can help maintain a healthy environment.")
            Can collaborate on a proposal for a project on ocean conservation (e.g., "We should reduce plastic use because it harms marine life and pollutes the oceans.")</p>
        </td>
    </tr>
</table>

<h2 style="color: #1a5276;">The Six Action-Oriented Approach (AOA) Lesson Stages</h2>
<table style="border: 1px dashed #333; border-collapse: collapse; width: 100%; margin: 10px 0;">
    <thead>
        <tr style="border: 1px dashed #333;">
            <th style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px; width: 33%; background-color: #f2f2f2;">Stage & Description</th>
            <th style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px; width: 33%; background-color: #f2f2f2;">Activities & Teacher Actions</th>
            <th style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px; width: 33%; background-color: #f2f2f2;">Estimated Date and Time</th>
        </tr>
    </thead>
    <tbody>
        <tr style="border: 1px dashed #333;">
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <h3 style="color: #1a5276; margin-top: 0;">STAGE 1 — Warm-up / Pre-task</h3>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">Engagement, Modeling and Clarification</p>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Warm-up:</b> The teacher displays a world map highlighting Panama's coastal areas and images of diverse Panamanian marine life (e.g., *coral reefs*, *mangroves*, *fish*, *sharks*). Students are asked to brainstorm and write down 3-4 words or short phrases they remember from previous lessons related to "Ocean Life and Conservation" and "The Animals in Our Waters." (5 min)</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Modeling:</b> The teacher writes a simple sentence on the board: "We *should protect* our *fragile coral reefs* because they are home to many *species*." The teacher models pronunciation, emphasizing the stress in "fragile" and "species." The teacher also models how to brainstorm ideas for writing about conservation. (3 min)</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Clarification (CCQs):</b>
                <ul>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">"Are coral reefs important for *marine life*?" (Expected answer: Yes, very important.)</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">"If a beach is *polluted*, is it good for *ocean animals*?" (Expected answer: No, it is harmful.)</li>
                </ul>
                (2 min)</p>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: center; margin: 0;">(10 min)</p>
            </td>
        </tr>
        <tr style="border: 1px dashed #333;">
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <h3 style="color: #1a5276; margin-top: 0;">STAGE 2 — Presentation</h3>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">Input of new language/content connected to Scenario: "Ocean Life and Conservation".</p>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Material:</b> The teacher provides students with a handout containing a sample formal letter (genre: formal letter, register: formal, format: printed text) from a student to a local environmental organization in Panama, requesting information about a marine conservation project (e.g., *Isla Iguanas coral reef restoration*). The letter explicitly uses *Present Simple* for facts, *Present Continuous* for ongoing actions, and *Modals* for suggestions/requests, along with key vocabulary such as *conservation, marine life, ecosystem, protect, endangered, polluted, sustain, biodiversity*. The teacher highlights key formal phrases. (5 min)</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Communicative Competence:</b> The student can produce clear and coherent written texts on familiar topics related to marine conservation, structuring paragraphs logically and using appropriate formal language to convey a request for information.</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Comprehension tasks:</b>
                <ul>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>Gist question:</b> "What is the main purpose of this letter?" (Expected answer: To request information about a conservation project.)</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>Information Matching:</b> Students match formal phrases from the letter (e.g., "I am writing to inquire about...", "I look forward to hearing from you") with their informal equivalents (e.g., "I want to know about...", "Hope to hear back soon").</li>
                </ul>
                (5 min)</p>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: center; margin: 0;">(10 min)</p>
            </td>
        </tr>
        <tr style="border: 1px dashed #333;">
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <h3 style="color: #1a5276; margin-top: 0;">STAGE 3 — Preparation / Practice</h3>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">Accuracy focus on Writing.</p>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Communicative Skill & Content:</b> Focusing on writing clear, grammatically correct sentences using *Present Simple* (describing ocean life), *Present Continuous* (ongoing actions like *polluting*), and *Modals* (*should, must*) for suggestions and obligations. Target vocabulary includes: *ocean, marine life, ecosystem, pollution, habitat, biodiversity, conservation, protect, conserve, preserve, sustainable, endangered, fragile*.</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Detailed Procedure:</b>
                <ol>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>Controlled Practice (Sentence Completion):</b> Students complete sentences related to marine conservation using appropriate forms of verbs and target vocabulary. Example: "The *coral reefs* ___ (be) home to many *species*. We ___ (should/must) *protect* them from *pollution*." (5 min)</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>Semi-controlled Practice (Guided Sentence Construction):</b> The teacher provides sentence beginnings related to the scenario, and students expand them into full, grammatically correct sentences, using given vocabulary and structures. Examples: "I am writing to inquire about...", "Our *coastal areas* are facing...", "We *should stop littering* to...", "Many *endangered species* ___." (5 min)</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>Pair Interaction (Peer Check on Formal Phrases):</b> In pairs, students receive a list of informal sentences (e.g., "I wanna know about...") and work together to rewrite them into formal phrases suitable for a letter, focusing on politeness and specific vocabulary. They then exchange their rewritten phrases with another pair for peer checking. (5 min)</li>
                </ol></p>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: center; margin: 0;">(15 min)</p>
            </td>
        </tr>
        <tr style="border: 1px dashed #333;">
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <h3 style="color: #1a5276; margin-top: 0;">STAGE 4 — Performance / Production</h3>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">Communicative task where students USE the language in a realistic scenario: "Ocean Life and Conservation".</p>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Communicative task:</b> Students will collaboratively draft a formal letter to a local Panamanian marine conservation organization or contribute to a proposal for a class project on ocean conservation.</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Task format:</b> Students work in small groups (3-4 students). Each group chooses one of the following writing tasks:
                <ul>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>Option 1 (Formal Letter):</b> Draft a formal letter requesting information about volunteering opportunities or specific projects (e.g., "Request details about volunteering for an *ocean cleanup event* at *Veracruz Beach* or participating in a *coral reef restoration project* at *Isla Iguanas*.").</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>Option 2 (Project Proposal Section):</b> Write a section of a class project proposal titled "Protecting Our Waters: A 9th Grade Initiative." This section should outline 2-3 specific actions the class could take (e.g., "We *should reduce plastic use* because it harms *marine life* and *pollutes the oceans*." or "We *must establish* awareness *Public Service Announcements (PSAs)* to educate our community about *biodiversity preservation*."). The section should also briefly describe the ecological importance, for example: "We *should mention* how *protecting marine life* can help maintain a *healthy environment*."</li>
                </ul>
                The teacher circulates, providing support and reminding students to incorporate *Present Simple, Present Continuous, Modals*, and the provided *vocabulary* (e.g., *endangered, vast, underwater, sustainable*). (10 min)</p>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: center; margin: 0;">(10 min)</p>
            </td>
        </tr>
        <tr style="border: 1px dashed #333;">
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <h3 style="color: #1a5276; margin-top: 0;">STAGE 5 — Assessment / Post-task</h3>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">Formative assessment strategy (observation checklist, rubric, or peer assessment).</p>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Formative assessment:</b> The teacher uses an observation checklist during group work, focusing on collaboration and appropriate use of target language. Students engage in peer assessment of the written product.</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Assessment Task:</b> Each group exchanges their draft letter or project proposal section with another group. Using a simple rubric, students provide constructive feedback on clarity, formality, grammar, and vocabulary usage. (5 min)</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Evaluation Criteria:</b>
                <ul>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">Clarity of request/proposal: Is the main purpose easy to understand?</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">Use of formal language and tone appropriate for the task.</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">Correct application of *Present Simple*, *Present Continuous*, and *Modals* for suggestions and obligations.</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">Integration of at least 5-7 *target vocabulary* words from the theme (e.g., *conservation, endangered, ecosystem, biodiversity, sustainable, protect, marine life*).</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">Collaborative effort: Evidence of shared contributions to the writing.</li>
                </ul>
                (3 min)</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Student Self-Assessment / Exit Ticket:</b> Students answer the following prompts individually:
                <ul>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">"What was one new formal phrase I learned and used today?"</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">"What part of writing a formal letter/proposal do I need to practice more?"</li>
                </ul>
                (2 min)</p>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: center; margin: 0;">(10 min)</p>
            </td>
        </tr>
        <tr style="border: 1px dashed #333;">
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <h3 style="color: #1a5276; margin-top: 0;">STAGE 6 — Reflection</h3>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Student self-reflection:</b>
                <ul>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">"How did our group ensure everyone's ideas were included and respected when writing the letter/proposal?"</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">"What specific feedback did I receive from my peers that will help me improve my formal writing about *ocean conservation*?"</li>
                </ul></p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Teacher reflection:</b> What aspects of formal writing (structure, tone, specific phrases) proved most challenging for the students? How effectively did they integrate the target grammar (*present simple, continuous, modals*) and *vocabulary* from the *scenario*? What adjustments or additional scaffolds (e.g., more sentence frames, explicit formal language mini-lessons, clearer models for *PSAs*) are needed for the next writing tasks, especially for the final project?</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Connection to next lesson:</b> The draft formal letters or project proposals written today will be refined and integrated into a larger, multi-modal project during Lesson 5 (Mediation & Integration), possibly as part of a presentation or an informational pamphlet about Panamanian marine *conservation*.</p>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: center; margin: 0;">(5 min)</p>
            </td>
        </tr>
    </tbody>
</table>